What problem is LPS solving?

Imagine you're searching for a word inside a big book — like searching for "AABAA" inside a giant string of letters.
The naive way is: try matching at position 0, fail, slide over by 1, try again, fail, slide by 1 again... This is slow.

The LPS array is a shortcut.
It's used in the KMP (Knuth-Morris-Pratt) pattern matching algorithm. 
It answers one question for every position in your pattern (the word you're searching for):

"If I fail to match here, how much of what I've already matched can I reuse, instead of starting over from scratch?"

It's called "Longest Prefix that is also a Suffix" — for every point in the pattern, 
it finds the longest chunk at the beginning of the pattern that also appears at the end of what you've matched so far.

Look at index 8 (the second "B"): everything from the start up through that point is "AABAABAA".
The last 3 letters "AAB" are identical to the first 3 letters of the whole pattern. So the LPS value there is 3 — meaning 
"if matching fails right after this, don't start over, just resume as if you'd already matched 3 letters."

