The key intuition to remember: the LPS array is basically a string comparing itself to itself.
Whenever a mismatch happens during pattern matching (whether against another string, or against itself while building the array),
instead of sliding back to the very beginning, you jump to lps[length - 1] — reusing the fact that part of what you already matched ,
happens to also be a valid starting chunk.

This is what makes KMP search run in linear time (O(n + m)) instead of the naive approach's worst case of O(n × m) —
you never re-examine a character in the main text twice.
